\subsection{Method}{

For each focal location, the function:
\enumerate{
\item Selects analog pool locations based on \code{select}, \code{max_clim}, \code{max_geog}, and \code{k}
\item Computes distance-based weights for each analog (via \code{weight} and \code{theta})
\item Fits a weighted least squares regression of \code{y} on \code{covariates}
using these weights, with optional ridge penalty \code{lambda}
\item Returns the regression coefficients (intercept + slopes)
}

The math: \verb{beta = (X'WX + lambda * I_p)^\{-1\} X'Wy}, where \code{W} is diagonal
weights, \code{X} is the design matrix (intercept + covariates), and \code{I_p} penalizes
covariate coefficients only (not the intercept).
}

\subsection{Relationship to Weighted Mean}{

With \code{lambda -> Inf}, covariate coefficients shrink to zero and the
intercept converges to the weighted mean. With \code{lambda = 0} (the default),
the full local regression is used. If covariates are centered (weighted
mean zero within each neighborhood), the intercept equals the weighted
mean at any lambda. The \code{lambda} parameter thus provides smooth
interpolation between a simple weighted average and a full local regression.
}

\subsection{Common Configurations}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Geographically weighted regression} (\code{select = "all"} or
\code{"knn_geog"}, with \code{max_geog} and geographic weights): Local spatial
regression using geographic proximity to define and weight neighborhoods,
equivalent to GWR.
\item \strong{Climate-nearest regression} (\code{select = "knn_clim"}, with
\code{max_geog} and \code{k}): Fixed-size neighborhoods of the k most similar
climates within geographic range.
}
}

\subsection{Prediction}{

The function returns coefficients only. Prediction at new covariate values
can be done via regression algebra, e.g.:

\preformatted{
prediction <- coef_intercept + coef_cov1 * covariate_1 + coef_cov2 * covariate_2
}
}
}
